Types of Threads

Very Important Interview Topic.

Two major types.

1. User-Level Threads (ULT)

Managed by user-space thread library.

Kernel doesn't know much about them.

Examples:

POSIX Threads library
Java Threads

Advantages:

✔ Fast creation

✔ Fast switching

Disadvantages:

If one thread blocks:

Entire Process May Block

depending on implementation.

2. Kernel-Level Threads (KLT)

Managed directly by OS kernel.

Kernel schedules them individually.

Advantages:

✔ Better scheduling

✔ Better concurrency

Disadvantages:

More overhead.

Thread vs Process

Very Important Interview Table.

Feature	Process	Thread
Resource Ownership	Own resources	Shares resources
Memory	Separate	Shared
Creation Cost	High	Low
Context Switch Cost	High	Low
Communication	Expensive	Easy
Isolation	Strong	Weak
Example

Suppose:

Chrome crashes.

If Chrome is a process:

Chrome Crashes

Only Chrome affected.

Spotify continues.

If a thread crashes inside Chrome:

Thread Problem

Often entire Chrome process may be affected.

Thread Communication

Easy because threads share memory.

Example:

Thread A
      ↓
Shared Memory
      ↑
Thread B

No complicated communication needed.

Benefits of Threads
1. Responsiveness

UI remains responsive.

Example:

Downloading file while browsing.

2. Resource Sharing

Threads share memory.

3. Economy

Creation cheaper than processes.

4. Better CPU Utilization

